Subject: VMS: read record files as text where Vim reads text in binary mode

Vim opens text files with READBIN ("rb") and splits the lines itself, in
:source, readfile(), get_cmd_output() (system() output, which DCL writes as
a variable-length record file), viminfo, help, "-s" scripts and others. On
VMS a binary stream on a VAR or VFC file returns the records without their
line ends, so every line runs into the next: a .vimrc made with EDIT or
DCL CREATE gives "E492: Not an editor command: set nocompatibleset ...".

Route mch_fopen() through vms_fopen(), which opens VAR and VFC files in text
mode when "rb" is asked for (the C RTL then ends each record with "\n"), and
everything else as asked. Vim's own binary files are stream files.

--- a/src/macros.h
+++ b/src/macros.h
@@ -163,7 +163,7 @@
 #ifdef VMS
 # define mch_access(n, p)	access(vms_fixfilename(n), (p))
 				// see mch_open() comment
-# define mch_fopen(n, p)	fopen(vms_fixfilename(n), (p))
+# define mch_fopen(n, p)	vms_fopen(vms_fixfilename(n), (p))
 # define mch_fstat(n, p)	fstat((n), (p))
 # undef HAVE_LSTAT		// VMS does not have lstat()
 # define mch_stat(n, p)		stat(vms_fixfilename(n), (p))
--- a/src/os_vms.c
+++ b/src/os_vms.c
@@ -219,6 +219,27 @@
     screen_start();
 }
 
+/*
+ * fopen() for mch_fopen().
+ * Vim reads text files in binary mode ("rb") and finds the line ends itself:
+ * :source, readfile(), the output of system(), viminfo, help and others.  A
+ * binary stream on a record file (variable length or VFC, as made by EDIT,
+ * DCL CREATE and most VMS programs) gives the records without line ends, so
+ * all lines would run into one.  Read those files as text: each record then
+ * ends with "\n".  Stream files, and so Vim's own swap, undo and spell files,
+ * are opened as asked.
+ */
+    FILE *
+vms_fopen(char *name, char *mode)
+{
+    stat_T	st;
+
+    if (STRCMP(mode, "rb") == 0 && stat(name, &st) == 0
+	    && (st.st_fab_rfm == FAB$C_VAR || st.st_fab_rfm == FAB$C_VFC))
+	return fopen(name, "r");
+    return fopen(name, mode);
+}
+
